/*! @arch_page arch-disagg-step-down-concurrency Async step-down concurrency synchronization model

# Summary \- Cursor operations and transactions

Every cursor operation makes two decisions that can be affected by an async step-down:

* Which layered table constituents should be accessed (stable only, both stable and ingest, or
  ingest only) and in what mode.
* Whether the transaction should be rolled back (this decision could also be made while trying to
  commit a transaction).

Schema operations also behave differently before and after the step-down TS is set.

Since the step-down timestamp can be set by a different thread with cursor or schema operations, it
requires synchronisation to guarantee certain visibility rules for other threads.

Transactions are expected to be rolled back when:

* A write transaction straddles the setting of a step-down timestamp.
* A read transaction that does not have a read timestamp straddles the step-down reconfiguration
  call.

Choosing the right constituents involves:

* Choosing the right write target: the stable table, both tables, or ingest only (the legacy write
  conflict detection mode or tables created after the step-down-ts is set).
  * It is simpler in some sense because writes are not permitted during the step-down
    reconfiguration, so the follower epoch writes are guaranteed to see the follower connection
    state.
* Choosing the right read target, which could also be stable, ingest, or both stable and ingest.

The difference in schema operation behavior is:

* Schema create creates both ingest and stable constituents before the step-down was set and only
  the ingest table when the step-down timestamp is set. All the other schema/cursor APIs should be
  aware of this and work in accordance with it.

# The step-down timestamp logic and important concurrency events during reconfigure(role=follower)

The step-down timestamp is set during `set_timestamp(step-down-ts)` and later cleared during
`reconfigure(role=follower)`, after the node has been turned into a follower by setting the
corresponding global variable.

Before switching to follower mode, reconfigure(role=follower) also marks all the shared btrees
OUTDATED and READONLY and only then changes the corresponding global role variable for follower
mode. While doing so, it holds the handle list read lock and obtains exclusive eviction access for
each tree while marking it. It also holds the checkpoint and schema locks for the entire duration
of the role change.

It also holds the step-down lock, but only while clearing the step-down timestamp and schema epoch.

It also increases the role generation right before switching to follower mode.

The lock sequence looks like this:

```
Step-down reconfiguration {
    acquire checkpoint lock
    acquire schema lock
    acquire handle list read lock

    for each handle except the history store {
        if layered: clear the step-down-created flag
        if an open disaggregated btree that is not already read-only {
            obtain exclusive eviction access
            mark the btree read-only and its handle outdated
            release exclusive eviction access
        }
    }
    mark the shared history store read-only and outdated with exclusive eviction access
    advance the role generation
    publish follower role with a release store
    release handle list read lock

    acquire step-down write lock
    clear the step-down timestamp and schema epoch
    release step-down write lock

    release schema lock
    release checkpoint lock
}
```

# The schema ops part of the logic

The logic here is that if a table is created after the step-down timestamp was set, it should not
create the stable constituent (all such operations should be replicated on the new leader after it
gets promoted to create these tables in the cloud; after the step-down TS is set, only local copies
of these tables are created). In every place where schema is later created, verified, dropped, or
opened, it always holds the global schema lock, as it does when the step-down timestamp is set.

The interesting piece is that when a layered table is opened and it detects that the step-down-ts is
set and the stable table does not exist, it sets a special step-down-created flag on the dhandle,
which cursors later use to determine whether they should access the stable constituent. This flag is
also removed during the `reconfigure(role=follower)` call, when all the layered tables start working
in “follower” mode.

The step-down schema epoch is also cleared during reconfigure(role=follower).

# Transaction semantics related to ingest/stable

## Write transaction choices

For every write operation, the decision about which constituent to write a change to is based on the
following data:

* Whether a step-down TS is set or not set when the transaction begins
* Which role is read during the cursor operation
* Whether the dhandle has a flag marking that the table was created during the step-down window
* The mode (mirroring writes or legacy write conflict detection) the step-down is operating in

| Role | Step-down-created table | Step-down TS set at txn begin | Mirroring | Write target |
| ---- | ----------------------- | ------------------------- | --------- | ------------ |
| Follower | Either | Either | Either | Ingest only |
| Leader | Yes | Either | Either | Ingest only |
| Leader | No | No | Either | Stable only |
| Leader | No | Yes | Enabled | Both stable and ingest |
| Leader | No | Yes | Disabled | Ingest only |

To start with something simple, the write conflict detection mode is global and does not need a sync
mechanism.

Both setting the step-down timestamp and reading it during txn\_begin are protected by the same
global step-down lock. It’s enough to read the step-down timestamp value at the transaction
beginning only because write transactions are expected to be stopped before
reconfigure(role=follower), so a transaction can expect that if it observed the step-down timestamp
being set, it won’t change until the end of the transaction. Cursor operations can also rely on
transaction-begin data because all writes are required to have explicit transactions (no auto-txn).

For write transactions, the role being read does not have concurrent access because write
transactions are not allowed to run during reconfigure(role=follower), so the role change is
expected to be ordered by an application that stops serving writes before reconfigure and resumes
them only afterwards.

The flag indicating whether the table was created after the step-down timestamp was set follows the
same rules. Synchronisation for write transactions is not needed because no writes occur during
reconfigure(role=follower), and if a transaction or a cursor operation has seen the
step-down-created flag on a dhandle, it won’t be cleared until the end of the transaction. So an
operation can see either the step-down-created flag with the leader role, or the follower role
(after reconfigure) without the step-down-created flag.

Another peculiar thing is that all pre-reconfigure write transactions should access the stable table
in live-btree mode, while all follower transactions should access it only through a checkpointed
read-only view. This does not require any additional synchronisation either, since no write
transactions should be running during the reconfiguration.

## Read transaction choices

The most important point about read transactions is that only explicitly started transactions with
snapshot isolation or a read\_timestamp are allowed in disagg mode. This means that all the
transactions are expected acquire a snapshot (or get access to a read timestamp snapshot) and always
read this state without taking into account any later changes. Given that, we will split the
analysis into different subcategories based on when the transaction started and, therefore, acquired
a snapshot.

Since both reading the step-down timestamp value and writing it obtain the same lock, we can
definitely split read transactions into those that started before the step-down timestamp was set
and those that started after. Another separation could be between transactions that started before
the reconfiguration and those that started after. This separation is guaranteed by the fact that WT
first changes the role field value from leader to follower and only then unsets the step-down
timestamp under the step-down lock, so every transaction will see either step-down-ts=N and
role=leader/follower (step-down transition mode) or step-down-ts=NONE and role=follower (follower
mode). Clearing the timestamp under the step-down write lock orders it against the begin-time read:
a transaction that observes the cleared timestamp must also see that the node is now a follower.

### Pre-step-down-ts txns

Such transactions record that the step-down timestamp was not set at the beginning, so they can
continue reading only the stable constituent throughout the entire step-down until they observe
effects or reconfigure(role=follower). Accessing or not accessing the ingest table in their case
will change nothing, since ingest table writes will always happen after the step-down TS is set, so
they will always be empty for a snapshot acquired by such a transaction.

The tricky moment with such transactions is that as soon as they straddle
`reconfigure(role=follower)`, they should start accessing the stable table via a read-only
checkpointed view. Since there are no locks around changing the global role field from leader to
follower, there is no guarantee that all read transactions will see it as soon as it’s set (some
transactions might be in progress at this moment or pass the point where they check the value), but
it’s fine, since the step-down reconfiguration marks all the stable tables as readonly, which gives
eviction all the needed signals, so it’s fine to reopen the stable table eventually in this case.

However, only read transactions with a read\_ts provided can safely reopen the stable table in this
case, since the snapshot they are reading is protected by the HS. All read transactions that
straddle the step-down reconfigure and don’t have a read-ts are rolled back \- see below.

### Post-step-down-ts txns

This is the class of transactions that observe both the step-down-ts set and the role being leader.
To survive the step-down reconfigure, they should start accessing the ingest table for reads, since
not all the needed writes are included in the concluding checkpoints (some of them might already be
accessible only via ingest tables after the reconfigure, since they were committed after the
step-down TS was set).

To keep the state between the acquired snapshot and the step-down-ts value acquired at the
beginning, WT first acquires a snapshot and then reads the step-down TS protected by a lock. In this
case, there will never be a situation where a cursor sees role=leader and the step-down TS is not
set, but the snapshot is new enough to read ingest writes.

These transactions can access a table created after the step-down timestamp was set, provided
creation preceded transaction begin. Such a table has only an ingest constituent. While its
step-down-created flag is set, a leader cursor skips opening stable (see the schema operations
section above). Transactions that begin after reconfiguration can also access it.

However, this flag is cleared during the step-down reconfiguration before the role is changed. Since
cursor ops do not hold any locks on the fast path, it may well be that a cursor operation racing
with clearing the flag during the step-down reconfigure will not see the step-down-created flag but
will still observe the leader role being stored. In this case, the cursor op will try to open the
stable table, but since both opening the stable table and the step-down reconfiguration acquire the
global schema lock, opening the stable table will need to wait for the step-down reconfiguration to
complete and will also be guaranteed to see the role as follower afterwards because of the mutex
semantics. In this case, being unable to open such a table will be tolerated, and all subsequent
cursor operations will be guaranteed to see the follower state, where it is totally fine to be
unable to open a stable table (the checkpoint has not been picked up yet).

The role and the table flag must agree once a cursor observes follower. Cursor operations
therefore acquire-load the role before reading the flag, and
reconfiguration release-stores follower only after clearing it. An operation that sees follower
must also see the cleared flag, although an operation already in progress can still use the old
role and encounter the race described above.

### Post-reconfigure txns

Read transactions whose snapshots are established in follower mode can read the follower view and
need no step-down-related extra synchronisation. If snapshot acquisition observes follower and
validates the recorded role generation, the transaction's cursor operations are guaranteed to also
observe follower.

## Cursor opens racing with reconfiguration

Cursor operations that race with reconfiguration need separate consideration.

During reconfiguration, stable trees are marked read-only and their handles outdated before
follower is published. A cursor operation can therefore observe the outdated flag on the stable
handle while the connection role read for that operation is still leader. Ingest remains accessible
throughout the transition, but a new live stable open must not succeed once the node is a follower.

This case is handled because a fresh btree open takes the schema lock, as does the step-down
reconfiguration. If reconfiguration wins, the open waits to acquire the lock and then observes the
updated follower role. The cursor operation returns `WT_ROLLBACK` rather than opening a live stable
tree. If the application retries the transaction, it will see the follower role.

If a live stable open that completes before reconfiguration it is a "post-step-down-ts" transaction;
see the previous section for more details.

## Rolling back straddlers

### Write transactions straddling setting the step-down timestamp

The check that guarantees rolling back a write straddler is at transaction commit. Transaction
begin and the commit-time check read whether the step-down timestamp is set under the same
step-down read lock, while setting the timestamp takes the write lock. A write transaction is
rolled back if the timestamp was not set when it began but is set at the commit-time check.
Cursor write operations can also roll back a straddler early, but these checks are purely a
performance optimization: they do not acquire the lock and do not establish the happens-before
edge that guarantees detection at commit.

The lock is released before the rest of the commit, so the check, not commit completion, determines
whether the transaction straddles the setting. A transaction that passes the check can therefore
finish committing after the step-down timestamp is set. This is safe provided the application
guarantees that this stable-writing transaction's commit timestamp is at or below the step-down
timestamp, N.

### Read transactions without read ts straddling the step-down reconfiguration

Read transactions without a read timestamp that span reconfiguration must be rolled back.
Their snapshot was acquired against the live btree and
cannot safely describe the read-only checkpoint view. The differences in transaction-ID
representation behind this are outside the scope of this document.

To detect such transactions, snapshot acquisition at transaction begin enters the current role
generation and records the role in the session. Reconfiguration advances the generation before
publishing follower. A cursor operation that opens or reopens stable compares the role and
generation with those recorded for the snapshot. If the transaction has no read timestamp and
either differs, the operation returns `WT_ROLLBACK`. See @ref generations for the generation
semantic and synchronisation guarantees.

Comparing the generation also catches a step-down that advanced the generation before publishing
follower. The follower role is release-stored after the advance, so a cursor that acquire-loads
follower must also see the advanced generation.

A transaction that races with reconfiguration while acquiring its snapshot has not yet read under
that snapshot. Snapshot acquisition checks the recorded role and generation after the scan and
rebuilds the snapshot if either changed, rather than rolling back the transaction.

Transactions with a read timestamp do not need the role-and-generation check when opening or
reopening stable because the history store protects their reads. Their timestamp must still be
at least the latest published checkpoint's oldest timestamp, which is no older than that of the
checkpoint being opened. A newer checkpoint pickup during the open can therefore make this
requirement stricter.

## Eviction synchronisation

Setting the read-only flag does not by itself stop eviction already in progress from writing shared
pages. Reconfiguration therefore obtains exclusive eviction access
for each open disaggregated tree that is not already read-only, marks the tree read-only and its
handle outdated, then re-enables eviction. Ingest is not marked. The shared history store is
processed last so eviction can still use it while draining the other trees.

Exclusive eviction access prevents new normal eviction for the tree, interrupts and clears its
walk, removes queued candidates, and waits for concurrent LRU eviction to finish. Eviction remains
disabled until the tree is marked read-only and outdated.

Readers may still be using the old stable tree after it is marked. Eviction must retain any page
whose content cannot be read back from shared storage while the old handle is still in use. Once
the last reader releases the handle, eviction can discard the remaining pages without reconciling
them to shared storage.

## Detecting write conflicts synchronisation

### Why ingest-only writes need a cross-table check

The @ref disagg-roles-step-down overview describes write routing. Without mirroring, writers in
different constituents do not share an update chain, so a single-tree conflict check cannot detect
a conflicting update in the other constituent.

An ingest-routed write therefore checks the key in both constituents when the transaction observed
the step-down timestamp set at begin.

Mirrored writes do not need this cross-table check because writing stable already uses the usual
stable-tree conflict check.

### Stable transactions versus ingest transactions

Consider a transaction that passes its commit-time straddler check before the timestamp is set,
and another that begins with the timestamp set. The first writes stable and can commit; the second
writes ingest. Their writes are ordered as follows:

1. The stable transaction publishes its writes before its read-locked commit-time check.
2. The timestamp setter acquires the write lock after that check releases the read lock.
3. The ingest transaction takes the read lock after the timestamp store and records the set flag.
4. Its cursor operations run after that begin-time read.

The timestamp setter's write lock connects the two read-locked sections; read locks alone would not
order them. The ingest transaction must therefore see the stable updates when checking the update
chains. However, passing the stable transaction's check does not mean its commit has completed.
The ingest transaction acquires its snapshot before reading the step-down timestamp under the lock,
so that snapshot may still exclude the stable transaction, even if it has committed by the time
the locked timestamp read finishes. The ingest write must therefore check stable for a conflict:
an ordinary lookup can miss an update excluded by the snapshot, and the native ingest conflict
check cannot inspect that update in stable.

### Ingest transactions versus straddlers

A straddler begins before the timestamp is set and reaches its commit-time check afterwards.
Its cursor write checks can miss the setting, so it may still write stable while another
transaction writes ingest. No lock ordering guarantees that either sees the other's writes.

Both may temporarily write the same logical key into different constituents, but the locked
commit-time check must roll back the straddler, so they cannot both commit. Other snapshot readers
cannot see its uncommitted writes, and rollback marks them aborted. If the ingest transaction does
see one of these updates, its conflict check may roll it back too; this is safe even though the
straddler must also roll back. Transactions writing the same constituent continue to use the usual
single-tree conflict check.


# Exceptional operations and model limits

* Prepared commits while the step-down timestamp is set are unsupported on the normal path; the
  internal-testing debug-mode bypass does not guarantee support for prepared straddlers.
  Cross-table conflict checks skip on-page prepared time windows, matching leader behavior; they do
  not add support for prepared transactions across step-down.
* The largest-key operation ignores snapshot visibility by contract and always consults ingest,
  regardless of the role or the begin-time step-down flag.
* Transactions are expected to access tables created before they began. Access to a newer table is
  outside this model. During reconfiguration, a transaction older than a step-down-created table
  can resolve neither constituent as the table flag is cleared before follower is published. An
  always-on assertion detects the missing constituent; this is not a supported routing exception.
* Snapshot reset is outside this model during async step-down. It replaces the snapshot without
  refreshing the begin-time step-down flag. Resetting a pre-step-down transaction's snapshot after
  the timestamp is set can therefore include ingest writes while cursor reads still select stable
  only. If the application needs a new snapshot, it can end the transaction and begin a new one
  instead. The current API rejects reset outside snapshot isolation or after modifications, but
  has no step-down-specific rejection. Enforcing this model restriction remains pending.

